    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data for a new low-fat mayo formulation and hitting some stability issues in our accelerated aging tests. The pH is quite low (around 3.5) and we're seeing significant phase separation after 48 hours. Can you walk me through the specific role lecithin plays in stabilizing the oil-water interface in these high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a natural emulsifier, is crucial for stabilizing your oil-water interface, especially in a low-pH environment like yours. It acts as a surfactant, meaning it has both hydrophilic (water-loving) and lipophilic (oil-loving) parts. These molecules orient themselves at the interface, forming a protective, robust barrier around the oil droplets, which prevents them from coalescing and causing phase separation. In high-acid conditions, the structure of this interface is under stress, and the lecithin molecules effectively form a steric and electrostatic hindrance that keeps the droplets dispersed.",
      "source": "target",
