    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If the electrostatic repulsion is failing, maybe we need more than just lecithin. Should we be looking at adding a secondary stabilizer, like a polysaccharide or a modified starch, to help provide better steric stabilization? Or is there a way to modify the lecithin itself to be more acid-resistant?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adding a secondary stabilizer, like a polysaccharide or modified starch, is a very sound strategy to bolster your emulsion's defense. These polymers can adsorb onto the oil droplets, creating a thick, physical layer—a steric barrier—that physically prevents the droplets from getting close enough to merge, which is exactly what you need when electrostatic forces are weak. You could also look into using a gum that has a higher molecular weight, which provides a more substantial physical cushion. This is often a more reliable fix than trying to chemically engineer the lecithin itself to withstand the acid.",
      "source": "target",
